    "title": "Read across the water levels",
    "reading": ["The repeated windmills give Kinderdijk a powerful visual order, but the research drawing needs to cut across that image. A section relating drained land, waterways, storage and pumping would explain why the components occupy their positions. The landscape's visual continuity should not be mistaken for a single undifferentiated water surface.", "The relationship to Wisboomgemaal is especially useful. Read together, the landscape and pumping-station entries allow the research to distinguish the continuing task of drainage from the technologies used to carry it out. The precise contemporary operating sequence still needs documentation."],
    "takeaway": "Kinderdijk supports a systems question: how does land remain usable when its habitability depends on work beyond each parcel? For development research, the key issue is the continuing collective commitment that connects individual land use to water-level management and infrastructure renewal.",
